    "recent_exchanges": [
      {
        "user": "I'm checking Medicare coverage for myself in Collier County. I'd like to keep my cardiologist and understand what visits could cost. Where should I start?",
        "assistant": "A good place to start is with the two big decisions tied to your goals: keeping your cardiologist and understanding visit costs. Medicare has two main paths: Original Medicare, which is Parts A and B through the federal program, or Medicare Advantage, which is a private plan that provides Parts A and B. With Original Medicare, you may add a separate drug plan and possibly Medigap; with Medicare Advantage, most plans include drug coverage. In either path, the Part B premium still applies. For costs, look at both the monthly premium and what you pay when you use care, like deductibles, copays, or coinsurance. To compare plans in your area, I need to clear up one detail first.",
        "question": "What ZIP code do you live in? Plans differ by area, and this helps focus the comparison where you want coverage."
      },
      {
        "user": "I currently have Aetna Medicare Advantage. My cardiologist is Dr. Maya Reed at Cypress Heart Clinic in Naples. I don't know the exact plan name.",
        "assistant": "That gives us a useful starting point. You’ve reported that you’re currently in a Medicare Advantage plan, and not knowing the exact Aetna plan name is normal for a first pass. For your cardiologist and visit costs, the key checks are separate: the exact plan, the exact office location, and the specific kind of visit or test. A carrier name alone doesn’t confirm that the doctor is in-network for your plan or what you’d pay. Since plans vary by county, the next detail that helps narrow comparison is your ZIP code.",
        "question": "What ZIP code do you live in? Plans differ by county, and this helps focus the comparison area."
      }
    ],
    "current_message": "Before we go further, what does Medicare Advantage mean? Does the office accepting Aetna prove it takes my particular plan?",
    "evidence": [
      {
        "id": "advantage",
        "title": "Medicare Advantage",
        "text": "Medicare hospital and medical coverage through a private insurance company’s plan instead of Original Medicare. Most plans include drug coverage. Doctors, costs, and rules vary by plan. The Part B premium still applies; some plans help pay all or part of it. Check each plan's doctors, drug coverage, costs, and rules.",
        "summary": "A private plan provides Parts A and B. Most plans also include drug coverage.",
        "question": "What is Medicare Advantage?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "checks",
        "title": "What to check for each plan",
        "text": "Records are in source order or ordered by explicit published fields. Eligibility, doctor participation, exact medicines and personal total cost have not been established. Compare the exact plan's providers, drug coverage, costs, and enrollment requirements before making a decision.",
        "summary": "A plan list is the starting point. Check doctors, medicines, and costs for your situation.",
        "question": "What should I check before choosing a plan?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/plan-compare/"
      },
      {
        "id": "options",
        "title": "How your options fit together",
        "text": "There are two main routes. Original Medicare is Parts A and B; you can add a separate drug plan and may be able to buy Medigap to help with your share of costs. Medicare Advantage provides A and B through a private plan, and most plans include drug coverage. Medigap goes with Original Medicare, not Medicare Advantage. You still pay the Part B premium with either route.",
        "summary": "Start with hospital and medical care. Then compare the two ways to get your coverage.",
        "question": "How do my Medicare options fit together?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "routesintro",
        "title": "The two coverage routes",
        "text": "Original Medicare is Parts A and B through the federal program. Medicare Advantage is a private plan approved by Medicare that provides Parts A and B. Both routes provide Medicare coverage, with different rules and costs. We can add the other coverage pieces after this distinction is clear.",
        "summary": "You can get Parts A and B through the federal program or through a private Medicare Advantage plan.",
        "question": "What are the two ways to get Medicare?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "original",
        "title": "Original Medicare",
        "text": "Parts A and B together, run by the federal government and accepted by any U.S. doctor who takes Medicare. You can add separate drug coverage (Part D) and may be able to buy a Medigap policy for certain A/B costs.",
        "summary": "Parts A and B are the foundation. Other coverage can help with drugs and some costs.",
        "question": "What is Original Medicare?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "costs",
        "title": "Monthly cost and care costs",
        "text": "A premium is the amount you pay each month for coverage. A deductible is an amount you pay before coverage pays its share under the benefit's rules. Copays and coinsurance are costs when you use care. A plan's premium alone is not your total cost.",
        "summary": "A monthly premium and what you pay when you use care are different costs.",
        "question": "What costs should I compare?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs"
      }
